WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.411
TCP is stream-oriented.

2
00:00:01.411 --> 00:00:05.577
It provides an ordered byte stream, not application message boundaries.

3
00:00:05.577 --> 00:00:14.110
That means one receive operation may contain part of a message, exactly
one message, or pieces of multiple messages depending on timing and
buffering.

4
00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:18.948
Assuming that one receive call equals one complete message is unsafe and
unreliable.

5
00:00:18.948 --> 00:00:23.920
The application protocol must define how the receiver knows when a message
is complete.

6
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.086
UDP is message-oriented at a lower level, but it has different tradeoffs.

7
00:00:28.086 --> 00:00:34.737
Messages may be lost, duplicated, reordered, or arrive from unexpected
sources depending on the design and network.

8
00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:46.496
UDP can be appropriate for some workloads, but the application has to
account for duplicate messages, replay considerations at a high level,
size limits, and validation of peer identity and message content.

9
00:00:46.496 --> 00:00:49.721
The transport does not remove protocol responsibility.

10
00:00:49.721 --> 00:00:52.140
Data framing defines message boundaries.

11
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.373
Length-prefix framing can state how many bytes belong to the next message.

12
00:00:56.373 --> 00:00:59.732
Delimiter-based framing can mark the end of a text message.

13
00:00:59.732 --> 00:01:04.167
Fixed-size records, binary formats, and text protocols each have
tradeoffs.

14
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:14.178
Whatever the design, the receiver should enforce maximum sizes, handle
incomplete data, reject malformed frames, and avoid waiting forever for a
message that never finishes.

15
00:01:14.178 --> 00:01:17.134
Encoding and decoding are also security-relevant.

16
00:01:17.134 --> 00:01:21.502
A byte stream must become structured data before business logic can trust
it.

17
00:01:21.502 --> 00:01:28.154
Text encodings, binary fields, numeric ranges, command names, and optional
values should be validated explicitly.

18
00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:31.983
Protocol parsers should fail predictably when input is malformed.

19
00:01:31.983 --> 00:01:39.240
A clear framing and validation model prevents accidental trust in
incomplete, oversized, ambiguous, or unexpected messages.
